## Worked questions

Questions somebody actually asked, with the request that answers each. Copy the shape. Every value
in them is one this snapshot declares, and every one of them is inside the bounds above - the
period, the group-by count - because a bundle carrying one that is not does not load. So a question
below is one this deployment answers rather than one it would decline.

### business_revenue_in_the_north

- Asked as: "what are business customers in the north worth per month", "Umsatz der Firmenkunden im
  Norden"
- Send: metric `recurring_revenue`, grain `month`, period `2026-06-01` to `2026-07-01`, `segment` =
  `business`, `region` = `north`

### churn_rate_over_the_half_year

- Asked as: "how has the churn rate developed this year", "wie hat sich die Abwanderungsrate
  entwickelt"
- Send: metric `churn_rate`, grain `month`, period `2026-01-01` to `2026-07-01`

### revenue_by_segment_in_june

- Asked as: "how much recurring revenue did each segment bring in June", "wie viel wiederkehrender
  Umsatz kam im Juni pro Segment"
- Send: metric `recurring_revenue`, grain `month`, period `2026-06-01` to `2026-07-01`, grouped by
  `segment`

## Provenance, and what makes a number checkable

Every answer carries the version and the digest of the definitions that produced it. They are what
turn a number into a claim somebody can verify: the digest is taken over the parsed definitions, so
it moves when what a metric means changes and does not move when a document is merely reformatted.

Quote both whenever you report a figure, alongside the period and any filters applied. If two
answers in one conversation carry different digests, the definitions changed underneath you: say so
rather than presenting the two numbers as comparable.
